diff --git a/wfglink/docs/METHODS.md b/wfglink/docs/METHODS.md
index 5c9a4a8..ef5992f 100644
--- a/wfglink/docs/METHODS.md
+++ b/wfglink/docs/METHODS.md
@@ -22,6 +22,8 @@ This describes what wfglink computes and how. Every parameter named here lives i
    - `moments` keeps it; with [detect.streaks.refine] the moments are recomputed without a threshold inside a rectangle along the streak that reaches [detect.streaks.margin_sigma] PSF widths past its ends.
    - `erf` fits a model of the streak (`detect/streaks.py`). Luma is decoded with the power law [detect.streaks.erf.gamma] first, because the model is linear in light and video luma is gamma encoded (a tone curve flattens a streak's plateau relative to its edges and lengthens it). The value must match the camera's tone curve: 2.2 suits sRGB and Rec.709 video, 1 suits linear video, and a log or HDR profile needs its own value. A wrong value biases every length by a similar fraction, which the exposure estimator's proportionality check cannot see. The signed residual is sampled every [detect.streaks.erf.step_px] on a grid along the moment axis, without a threshold: [detect.streaks.erf.reach] times the moment length plus [detect.streaks.erf.margin_sigma] PSF widths long, [detect.streaks.erf.half_width_sigma] PSF widths to each side. The grid is collapsed into two profiles: across the axis (weighted along by the first estimate's extent), which is a Gaussian of the PSF width s, and along the axis (weighted across by that Gaussian, a matched filter), which is a box of length L blurred by the same Gaussian, F / L [Phi((u - c + L/2) / s) - Phi((u - c - L/2) / s)], two error-function edges. Both profiles are fitted jointly (Levenberg-Marquardt, s shared), so s comes from the across profile where the edges cannot separate it from L and from both where they can. Optionally the brightness may change linearly along the streak, with a Gaussian prior ([detect.streaks.erf.ramp_sigma]), and [detect.streaks.erf.iterations] = 2 refits once in a window centred on the first fit with the axis turned to the across centroids of the streak's two halves. A fit that leaves the frame, puts an edge outside its window or puts s at a bound ([detect.streaks.erf.sigma_range_px]) keeps the moment length; positions are not changed. The fit uses one frame only, so the axis is still the direction in which a noisy blob looks longest: for streaks shorter than about two PSF widths the length keeps an upward noise bias. Unlike the moment length, which does not depend on the axis, the fit measures along the detector's axis: when that axis is far from the motion (short or faint streaks), the length comes out short, down to zero, which means the profile along the axis is no wider than the PSF (the exposure estimator's screen drops such points). Saturated pixels are not excluded, so a clipped streak comes out long, as its moment length does; saturated detections are flagged and left out of the exposure fit and the streak speeds. A full 2D fit of the same model to the pixels (free centre and axis) gives similar lengths, with the same bias, at several times the cost.
 
+9. File mode on large frames: multi-scale detection ([detect.multiscale.enabled], `detect/multiscale.py`). The detector's scales (the top-hat kernel, the prefilter, the round-blob and streak-width limits, the mirror test's blocks) are in analysed pixels and were chosen on 720p video. In high-resolution video an ember close to the camera is much larger than that: a defocused streak 10 to 15 px wide is mostly removed by the top-hat, or masked as a flame, while the same ember at half the resolution is an ordinary detection. A blanket downscale would lose the far embers, which can be a pixel or two wide. So file mode also runs the detector on copies of the frame downscaled by 2 and by 4 (block averages after cropping to a multiple of the factor), at most [detect.multiscale.max_octaves] of them, each only while it keeps at least [detect.multiscale.min_level_pixels] pixels, and only on frames with at least [detect.multiscale.min_frame_pixels] pixels: a 720p frame is analysed at one scale, exactly as before, and the booth (which analyses at most 1280x720, section 9) never uses coarse levels. Each level has its own detector, with its own background, noise maps, mirror test and large-region mask in its own pixels. Coarse detections are converted to source pixels (a coarse pixel centre x is f x + (f - 1) / 2 for factor f; lengths and widths times f, areas, fluxes and second moments times f squared) and merged level by level onto the finer result. A coarse detection covers the finer detections within [detect.multiscale.footprint_sigma] of its PSF sigmas (at least [detect.multiscale.footprint_min_px]) of its axis segment; its PSF sigma in source pixels is f sqrt(l2 - prefilter^2), from the minor-axis second moment. The native detector measures an ember whole up to a PSF sigma of [detect.multiscale.native_max_sigma_px], a level of factor f up to f times that. A coarse detection too large for the next finer level replaces the finer detections it covers, which are pieces of it (rims the top-hat left) holding little of its light, unless together they hold at least [detect.multiscale.replace_max_flux_share] of its flux (summed signed residual): then the finer level measured the light whole, more sharply, or they are separate embers that merged at the coarse scale, and they stand. A smaller coarse detection defers to the finer ones it covers, so a far ember keeps its native measurement. A coarse detection that covers nothing is kept when its PSF sigma is at least [detect.multiscale.coarse_only_min_sigma_px] times the next finer level's factor: an ember too wide for that level (often one that level's large-region mask took, halo and all); a smaller one is point-like at that level, well within its range, and that level found nothing there. The merge uses the detector's moments; the streak method of step 8 then runs once, on the detections that are kept, each on the residual of the level that measured it. The tracker is unchanged and sees one detection per ember, in source pixels; the detections table records the level that measured each one (`scale`, 1 = native). Limits: embers that merge at a coarse level stay apart only where a finer level found them; a column of bright near embers whose halos join is masked as a large bright region at the finer levels and merges into one component too large for the round-blob and streak-width limits at the coarser ones, so it is lost at every scale; and the coarse levels accept bright structures up to 2 or 4 times wider than the native top-hat does, so glare a few times wider than an ember can pass there.
+
 ## 3. Tracking (`track/`)
 
 Constant-velocity Kalman filter per track with the true time step. Assignment by the Hungarian method on the squared Mahalanobis distance d2 gated at [track.gate_chi2], plus [track.angle_penalty_cost] when a streak's axis disagrees with the predicted motion by more than [track.angle_penalty_deg]. A new track's second detection is searched within max([track.birth_radius_px], [track.birth_length_factor] L) per frame; without an exposure estimate the length term is divided by [track.birth_duty_min], because a streak from a partial exposure is shorter than the frame-to-frame displacement. Confirmation after [track.confirm_hits] hits in [track.confirm_window] frames; coasting for at most [track.max_coast] frames. A track that moves slower than [track.static_speed_px_s] for [track.static_seconds] is a static light and is never counted.
